# Python caches / envs
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/

# Regenerable heavy artefacts under results/. Figures (.png) ARE tracked, since they are the
# deliverable; meshes and field dumps are not, because they are large and a script reproduces
# them (the ILI mesh rebuilds in ~10 s).
results/**/*.msh
results/**/*.h5
results/**/*.xdmf
results/**/*.npz
results/**/*.bp

# ...EXCEPT the ones that cost hours to produce. A forward solve is ~2.4 h; losing one to an
# untracked working tree already cost this project a full day's work once. ~44 MB each.
!results/ili_forward/channel_data_*.npz
# ...except the wide-domain record. That rationale rested on a solve costing hours; on the
# GPU this one took 12 min, and it backs a NULL result, so 42 MB of history buys nothing.
# The imaging caches and figures under results/compare/*widedomain* carry the evidence, and
# the README records the two commands that rebuild it.
results/ili_forward/channel_data_*_w165_*.npz

# Extracted k-Wave cases: only ~1 min to re-extract, but re-extraction needs data/derrell/,
# which is itself gitignored bulk. ~24 MB each.
!results/kwave_cases/*.npz

# Beamformed image caches: tiny, and metric_robustness.py reads them.
!results/compare/images_*.npz

# NEVER track the wavefield snapshot dumps. wavefield_snap_p20deg.npz is 476 MB - over
# GitHub's HARD 100 MB per-file limit, so a push carrying it is rejected outright. It rebuilds
# from a ~20 min degree-3 solve with --snapshots. Listed after the negations so it wins.
results/**/wavefield_*.npz

# Stray outputs if a script ever writes to the project root by mistake.
/*.xdmf
/*.h5
/*.bp
/*.pvd
/*.vtu
/*.png
/*.mp4

# Animations are regenerable and can be enormous - the first draft of the wavefield GIF was
# 64 MB, which git warns about and which no reviewer wants to clone. Render them with
# viz/wavefield_gif.py (--stride / --colors keep them to a few MB) and add a specific file
# with `git add -f` only when it is small enough to be worth tracking.
results/viz/*.gif

# Generated artifact pages carry ~10 MB of inlined base64 images. Rebuild them with
# viz/build_artifacts.py rather than tracking them.
results/artifacts/*.html
